WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.150
The late Robin Williams will go down as one
of the most celebrated and deeply loved actors

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:05.940
of all time.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.840
He got his start in stand-up comedy and he
never gave up his tendency to ad-lib, coming

00:00:10.849 --> 00:00:14.919
up with jokes, impressions, and whatever else
popped into his agile mind.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.520
Here are some of his funniest unscripted moments.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.080
The 1997 film Good Will Hunting undoubtedly
hosts a fantastic script.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.160
Matt Damon and Ben Affleck wrote it to give
themselves great roles, and they did just

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.750
that with their touching, poignant story about
a Harvard janitor turned math genius.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.300
They won the Oscar for Best Original Screenplay,
but an improvised moment by their co-star

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.270
may have just been the film's most memorable
scene.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.550
Robin Williams won an Oscar for his role as
Dr. Sean Maguire, a psychology professor and

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.340
therapist to Will Hunting.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.151
During one session, Sean discusses a hilarious
and intimate memory of his late wife.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.500
"My wife used to fart when she was nervous."

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.500
“She had all these wonderful little idiosyncrasies…”

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.800
"She used to fart in her sleep."

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.700
On the DVD commentary track, Affleck and Damon
revealed that Williams made up the monologue

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.020
on the spot, and that Damon's uproarious laughter
was genuine.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.560
In the 1987 movie Good Morning, Vietnam, Williams
stars as a wild disc jockey on Armed Forces

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.500
Radio in the middle of the Vietnam War.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.960
He angers his superiors by playing rock 'n'
roll and R&amp;B records and by speaking out about

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.800
the futility and heartbreak of war.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.020
The drama of wartime is filtered through Williams'
presence, especially his motormouth scenes

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.740
talking about the escalating conflict, the
news, himself, and the music.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:28.720
"Time for the news"

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.640
“We actually don’t have a teletype machine
that’s Fred Astaire dancing on a hot plate…”

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:35.300
"thank you Fred"

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.940
Williams' moments behind the mic feel just
like a real DJ's ramblings, and to maintain

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.320
that authentic feel, he was given free reign.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.260
In the film's production notes, producer Mark
Johnson stated,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.460
"He managed to create something new for every
single take."

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:54.100
"Ku Klux Klan sues cartoon show Casper the Friendly Ghost.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.680
It’s not fair that he gets to float and
we don’t.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.720
… Thank you very much."

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.500
When you absolutely have to be awake at a
certain time, you'd typically use a loud and

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.630
effective alarm clock.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.000
But no buzzer could wake someone up like the
booming, familiar voice of Robin Williams

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.720
bursting into a comedy routine.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.280
In 1988, NASA asked the comedian to make a
wake-up call to the crew of the space shuttle

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.560
Discovery, the first shuttle flight after
the 1986 Challenger tragedy.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.580
So in a nod to his Good Morning, Vietnam catchphrase,
he shouted:

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.600
"Good morning, Discovery.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.260
Rise and shine, boys.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.520
Time to start doing that shuttle shuffle.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.080
You know what I mean."

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.020
NASA then cut to a pre-recorded parody of
the Green Acres theme song with lyrics about

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:37.520
space.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.940
But the song isn't that funny.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.160
They should've just let the clearly thrilled
Williams keep going.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.140
1996's The Birdcage is an extremely silly
farce.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.340
The plot concerns a young man who brings home
his fiancé and her extremely straight-laced

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.960
parents to meet his two dads, Armand and Albert,
played by Williams and Nathan Lane.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.200
Albert is a drag performer, so he dresses
up as a matronly old woman for the big dinner

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:00.900
with the in-laws.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.780
Everything goes wrong, of course, particularly
when Armand and Albert's fake servant makes

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.660
a soup but no entree.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.140
Armand ducks into the kitchen to freak out
for a minute, then carry the soup pot back

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.440
into the dining room, only to slip and fall.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.400
Williams' sudden and spectacular tumble was
unexpected, but he rolls with the moment,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.780
not once breaking character.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.280
"Shut up!

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:21.780
It's okay.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:22.900
We're all right.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:23.780
It's fine.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:24.780
Just shut up, gdamn you!

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.240
It's all right, stop crying!

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.160
Gdamn you!

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.200
What are you standing there for?!

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:28.700
Go!"

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.200
To get a guest spot on The Tonight Show during
Johnny Carson's last week as host was a very

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:33.920
big deal.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:38.940
Set to retire after nearly 30 years as the
undisputed king of late night, Carson was

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.340
only going to invite his most favorite and
most entertaining guests.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:47.700
Robin Williams fit the bill, so he stopped
by Carson's final regular episode on May 21,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.200
1992.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:52.800
Williams roasted Carson with old man jokes,
hitting the stage with a rocking chair for

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.540
the retiring comedian, and yelling as if he
were hard of hearing.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.380
"We're going to Spago!"

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.140
“Don’t be afraid I bought you the new
LA Medic Alert bracelet.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:03.720
It says, “

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.660
"I've fallen and get the hell away from me."

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.220
Before winning an Oscar, Williams won several
Golden Globes.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.560
When he took home the prize for 1991's The
Fisher King, his acceptance speech was as

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.640
pure, perfect, and definitive as a Robin Williams
routine as could be.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.259
He began with a mildly suggestive joke about
the look of the trophy.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.380
There's nothing on it.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:22.880
How appropriate."

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.760
Then he segued into current events.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.140
"In the words of George Bush - Arigato Gizama"

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.240
And then he had some words for his Fisher
King co-stars before just going off the rails

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.330
in the most Robin Williams way possible.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.260
"I'm just thanking everybody now"
“I’d like to thank Dustin, Steven NO WRONG

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:43.900
MOVIE!

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:44.400
Back up!"

